{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CetPJ7TKjrqf2VsLedPrx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have enough information. Let me document my analysis findings:\n\n**Repository findings:**\n- Order model at `sample-service/src/main/kotlin/com/unityinflow/sample/order/Order.kt:11-16` — no status field\n- OrderController at `sample-service/src/main/kotlin/com/unityinflow/sample/order/OrderController.kt:17-46` — POST create and GET endpoints\n- OrderRepository at `sample-service/src/main/kotlin/com/unityinflow/sample/order/OrderRepository.kt:11-27` — in-memory ConcurrentHashMap store\n- Shipment already has status at `sample-service/src/main/kotlin/com/unityinflow/sample/shipment/Shipment.kt:9-14`\n- ShipmentRepository at `sample-service/src/main/kotlin/com/unityinflow/sample/shipment/ShipmentRepository.kt:11-27` — no method to query by orderId\n- ErrorCode enum at `sample-service/src/main/kotlin/com/unityinflow/sample/api/ApiError.kt:31-37` — needs new codes\n- Exception handlers at `sample-service/src/main/kotlin/com/unityinflow/sample/api/ApiExceptions.kt` — ResourceNotFoundException (404), ConflictException (409)\n\n**Conventions in this repository:**\n- Controllers throw `ApiException` subclasses; `GlobalExceptionHandler` converts them to `ApiError` envelope\n- Error response envelope: `{ \"error\": { \"code\": \"ErrorCode\", \"message\": \"...\", \"fields\": [] } }`\n- Tests assert response *body* and error codes, not just HTTP status (per testing-and-verification skill)\n- Tests make a separate GET request after state changes to verify persistence\n- In-memory repositories with no ORM\n\n**Affected files (cannot be avoided):**\n1. **Order.kt** — Must add OrderStatus enum and status field to Order data class\n2. **OrderController.kt** — Must add `POST /orders/{orderId}/cancel` endpoint\n3. **OrderRepository.kt** — May need method to find shipments by orderId (to validate CONFIRMED status)\n4. **ShipmentRepository.kt** — Must add method to find shipments by orderId\n5. **ShipmentController.kt** — Must validate that shipments cannot be created for CANCELLED orders\n6. **ApiError.kt** — Must add new ErrorCode values for cancellation logic\n7. **OrderControllerTest.kt** — Must add tests for cancel endpoint with all cases\n8. **ShipmentControllerTest.kt** — Must add test for rejection when creating shipment for CANCELLED order\n\n**Risks:**\n- Adding status field to Order breaks existing creation code if not handled correctly\n- All-or-nothing guarantee: must not partially update state if CONFIRMED shipment exists\n- Idempotent retry requirement: must not error when cancelling already-CANCELLED order\n- ShipmentRepository needs access to orderId queries which weren't previously needed\n- Existing tests might fail if they don't expect status field in Order responses\n\n**Open questions:**\n- None; ticket is sufficiently detailed on all cases.\n\nNow proceeding to DESIGN phase.\n\n```\n<<PHASE:DESIGN>>\n```\n\n**Proposed changes, file by file:**\n\n1. **Order.kt**: Add OrderStatus enum (ACTIVE, CANCELLED) and add status field to Order data class with default ACTIVE for new orders.\n\n2. **OrderRepository.kt**: Add method `fun findByOrderId(orderId: String): List<Shipment>` to find all shipments by orderId. Wait, actually this belongs in ShipmentRepository since Shipment is the entity. OrderRepository doesn't need changes.\n\n3. **ShipmentRepository.kt**: Add method `fun findByOrderId(orderId: String): List<Shipment>` to query shipments by the orderId field.\n\n4. **ApiError.kt**: Add two new ErrorCode values: `ORDER_CANNOT_BE_CANCELLED` (for when CONFIRMED shipment blocks cancel) and optionally `ORDER_IS_CANCELLED` (for when trying to create shipment for cancelled order).\n\n5. **OrderController.kt**: Add endpoint `POST /orders/{orderId}/cancel` that:\n   - Throws ResourceNotFoundException if order doesn't exist (404)\n   - Checks if any shipment for this order has status CONFIRMED\n   - If CONFIRMED exists, throws ConflictException (409) with ORDER_CANNOT_BE_CANCELLED code\n   - If order already CANCELLED, returns success (idempotent)\n   - If order is ACTIVE, transitions to CANCELLED and cancels all CREATED shipments\n   - Returns the updated order\n\n6. **ShipmentController.kt**: Modify `create` endpoint to:\n   - After checking for duplicate shipmentId, check if order exists and is CANCELLED\n   - If order is CANCELLED, throw ConflictException (409)\n   - Otherwise proceed with creation as before\n\n7. **OrderControllerTest.kt**: Add tests:\n   - Test cancelling an ACTIVE order succeeds and transitions to CANCELLED\n   - Test cancel returns the cancelled order in response body\n   - Test separate GET request shows order is CANCELLED (per skill requirement)\n   - Test cancelling already-CANCELLED order succeeds and changes nothing\n   - Test cancelling order with CONFIRMED shipment fails with 409 and nothing changes\n   - Test separate GET after failed cancel shows order still ACTIVE and shipment unchanged\n   - Test cancelling non-existent order fails with 404\n   - Test error envelope contains ORDER_CANNOT_BE_CANCELLED code for CONFIRMED case\n\n8. **ShipmentControllerTest.kt**: Add test:\n   - Test creating shipment for CANCELLED order fails with 409\n   - Test error envelope contains proper code\n   - Test separate GET on order shows it's still CANCELLED (no partial state change)\n\n**Alternative approaches considered and rejected:**\n\n1. **Using a Service layer**: Could create an OrderService with cancel logic, but existing code doesn't have service layer — controllers directly use repositories. Introducing it would be over-engineering and violates \"preserve existing architecture.\"\n\n2. **Storing shipments in Order**: Could embed shipments list in Order, but this breaks the separate Shipment entity model and makes the data model more complex. Current pattern is cleaner.\n\n3. **Database transactions**: Could use database-level transactions for atomicity, but repository is in-memory only. The current structure doesn't support distributed transactions, so we handle atomicity by checking state before any writes.\n\n4. **Separate cancel status**: Instead of CANCELLED, could have separate cancellationStatus. But simpler to use a single status field as per requirement.\n\n**Data and error flow:**\n\n| Case | Input | Read | Check | Write | Response | HTTP |\n|------|-------|------|-------|-------|----------|------|\n| Cancel ACTIVE → CANCELLED | orderId | Order, Shipments by orderId | No CONFIRMED shipments | Order.status = CANCELLED, CREATED shipments.status = CANCELLED | Updated Order | 200 |\n| Retry: Cancel CANCELLED | orderId | Order, Shipments | (no-op) | None | Same Order | 200 |\n| Blocked: CONFIRMED exists | orderId | Order, Shipments | Any CONFIRMED found | None | ApiError(ORDER_CANNOT_BE_CANCELLED) | 409 |\n| Not found: Order missing | orderId | Order | Not found | None | ApiError(ORDER_NOT_FOUND) | 404 |\n| Create shipment for CANCELLED order | shipmentId, orderId | Order | order.status == CANCELLED | None | ApiError(ORDER_IS_CANCELLED or similar) | 409 |\n\n**Test strategy:**\n\n1. **OrderControllerTest**:\n   - `test_cancel_active_order` — POST cancel, assert 200 and response body has CANCELLED status, then GET and verify status is CANCELLED\n   - `test_cancel_already_cancelled` — POST cancel twice, second call returns 200 with same response body, verify no state change\n   - `test_cancel_blocked_by_confirmed_shipment` — Create order with shipment, confirm shipment, POST cancel (409), GET order to verify still ACTIVE, GET shipment to verify still CONFIRMED\n   - `test_cancel_nonexistent_order` — POST cancel nonexistent, assert 404 with ORDER_NOT_FOUND code\n   - `test_cancels_created_shipments` — Create order with CREATED shipment, cancel order, GET shipment to verify it's CANCELLED\n\n2. **ShipmentControllerTest**:\n   - `test_cannot_create_shipment_for_cancelled_order` — Create and cancel order, try to create shipment (409), GET order to verify still CANCELLED\n\nEach test follows the skill pattern: assert response body of the call, then make a separate GET to verify actual state, and for failures, verify state didn't change.\n\nNow proceeding to IMPLEMENTATION phase.\n\n```\n<<PHASE:IMPLEMENTATION>>\n```\n\nLet me start by modifying the Order model to add status:"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1687,"cache_read_input_tokens":22785,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1687},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"dbc99b69-297b-4b9b-b5a0-21373c0934bd","uuid":"6c5f73ad-6a2a-417f-96a8-36f1342a4cec","timestamp":"2026-09-09T19:04:10.063Z","request_id":"req_011CetPJ6qrsYvn5aexZ8kQm"}
